          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The concept of
            %em traced monoidal category
            axiomatizes the structure on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
            for it to have a sensible notion of (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+trace"}> partial
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trace"} trace
            the way it exists canonically in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+closed+categories"} compact closed categories
            %em right
            or
            %em left
            traces, and are referred to as
            %em right traced
            or
            %em left traced
            respectively. A category with compatible right and left traces is called a planar traced category. If the right and left traces agree, then we call a planar traced category
            = succeed "." do
              %em spherical
          %p
            Graphically, the trace of an endomorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:A\to A
            is represented by the closed loop
          %p
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/trace.png", :width => "150px"}/
          %p
            The ambiguity in whether to close the loop to the right or to the left is codified in the difference between left and right traced categories. Every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pivotal+category"} pivotal category
            can be canonically made into a planar traced category by using its implicit distinguished morphisms to close loops. Pivotal categories which induce spherical traced structures are known as
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spherical+categories"} spherical categories
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/denotational+semantics"} denotational semantics
            of programming languages, the trace is used to model
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/recursion"} recursion
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/substructural+logic"} substructural
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fixed+point+operator"} fixed point operator
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            The original definition due to (
            %a{:href => "#JoyalStreetVerity96"}> Joyal-Street-Verity 96
            ) is stated in the setting of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/balanced+monoidal+categories"} balanced monoidal categories
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Selinger2011"} Selinger’s survey
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi.mathscript 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathscr{C}
            is said to be
            %em right traced
            if it is equipped with family of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/maps"} maps
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} tr
                    %mi R
                    %mi X
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mtext Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mtext hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\tr^X_R
                  \\,\colon\,
                  \\text{Hom}(A\otimes X,B\otimes X)
                  \\longrightarrow
                  \\text{hom}(A,B)
          %p
            for all
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi B
                    %mo ,
                    %mi X
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi.mathscript 𝒳
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A,B,X\in \mathscr{X}
          %ul
            %li#Tightening
              %p
                %strong Tightening
                (naturality in
                = succeed ")" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                        %mo ,
                        %mi B
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A,B
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                        %mo ,
                        %mi B
                        %mo ,
                        %mi C
                        %mo ,
                        %mi D
                        %mo ,
                        %mi X
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi.mathscript 𝒞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A,B,C,D,X\in\mathscr{C}
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi h
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi A
                        %mo →
                        %mi B
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h \colon A\to B
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi B
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi X
                        %mo →
                        %mi C
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon B\otimes X\to C\otimes X
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi C
                      %mo →
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g \colon C\to D
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} tr
                        %mi R
                        %mi X
                      %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi g
                      %mo ⊗
                      %msub
                        %mtext id
                        %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi f
                      %mo ∘
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi h
                      %mo ⊗
                      %msub
                        %mtext id
                        %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo =
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi g
                      %mo ∘
                      %msubsup
                        %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} tr
                        %mi R
                        %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi h
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\tr_R^X\big(
                      (g\otimes \text{id}_X)
                      \\circ
                      f \circ (h\otimes \text{id}_X)
                      \\big)
                      \\;=\;
                      g \circ \tr_R^X(f) \circ h
            %li
              %p
                %strong Sliding
                (naturality in
                = succeed ")" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                        %mo ,
                        %mi B
                        %mo ,
                        %mi X
                        %mo ,
                        %mi Y
                        %mo ∈
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A,B,X,Y\in
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi A
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi X
                        %mo →
                        %mi B
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi Y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon A\otimes X\to B\otimes Y
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi Y
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g \colon Y\to X
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} tr
                        %mi R
                        %mi X
                      %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mtext id
                        %mi B
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi g
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo =
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %msubsup
                        %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} tr
                        %mi R
                        %mi Y
                      %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                      %mi f
                      %mo ∘
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mtext id
                        %mi A
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi g
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\tr_R^{X}\big(
                      (\text{id}_B\otimes g) \circ f
                      \\big)
                      \\;=\;
                      \\tr_R^{Y}\big(
                      f \circ (\text{id}_A\otimes g)
                      \\big)
            %li
              %p
                %strong Vanishing
                For all
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                        %mo ,
                        %mi B
                        %mo ,
                        %mi X
                        %mo ,
                        %mi Y
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi.mathscript 𝒞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A, B, X, Y \in \mathscr{C}
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} tr
                        %mi R
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo =
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi f
                      %mo ,
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo ∀
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\tr^1_R(f)
                      \\;=\;
                      f,\,\, \forall \, f\colon A\to B
              %p and
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} tr
                        %mi R
                        %mrow
                          %mi X
                          %mo ⊗
                          %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo =
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %msubsup
                        %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} tr
                        %mi R
                        %mi X
                      %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                      %msubsup
                        %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} tr
                        %mi R
                        %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\tr^{X\otimes Y}_R(f)
                      \\;=\;
                      \\tr_R^X\big( \tr_R^Y(f) \big)
              %p
                for all
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi A
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi X
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi Y
                        %mo →
                        %mi B
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi X
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi Y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon A\otimes X\otimes Y\to B\otimes X\otimes Y
            %li
              %p
                %strong Strength
                For all
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                        %mo ,
                        %mi B
                        %mo ,
                        %mi C
                        %mo ,
                        %mi D
                        %mo ,
                        %mi X
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi.mathscript 𝒞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A,B,C,D,X\in \mathscr{C}
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi C
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi X
                        %mo →
                        %mi D
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon C\otimes X\to D\otimes X
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g \colon A\to B
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} tr
                        %mi R
                        %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi g
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo =
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi g
                      %mo ⊗
                      %msubsup
                        %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} tr
                        %mi R
                        %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\tr_R^X(g\otimes f)
                      \\;=\;
                      g \otimes \tr_R^X(f)
          %p Graphically, we fix the notation for right trace
          %p
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/right-trace-graphical.png", :width => "300px"}/
          %p The above axioms are described with the following pictures:
          %p
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/selinger-diagram-tracing.png", :width => "550px"}/
          %p
            In the presence of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braided+monoidal+category"} braiding
            (such as in a
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal category
            %em yanking
            axioms:
          %ul
            %li
              %strong Yanking:
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Tr
                      %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi β
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                        %mo ,
                        %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi id
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Tr^X(\beta_{X,X}) = id_X
              for all
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi.mathscript 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\in\mathscr{C}
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi β
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta:X\otimes X\to X\otimes X
            is the braiding.
          %p A left traced category is defined similarly, with a family of morphisms
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} tr
                    %mi L
                    %mi X
                  %mo :
                  %mtext Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi X
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mtext Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tr_L^X:\text{Hom}(X\otimes A,X\otimes B)\to \text{Hom}(A,B)
          %p satisfying symmetric axioms. We write the left trace graphically as
          %p
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/left-trace-graphical.png", :width => "300px"}/
          %p
            A planar traced category is a monoidal category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi.mathscript 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathscr{C}
            which is simultaneously left and right traced, such that the following axioms are satisfied:
          %ul
            %li
              %strong Interchange
              For all
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi B
                      %mo ,
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Y
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi.mathscript 𝒞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A,B,X,Y\in \mathscr{C}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi B
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:Y\otimes A\otimes X\to Y\otimes B\otimes X
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} tr
                    %mi R
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msubsup
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} tr
                    %mi L
                    %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msubsup
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} tr
                    %mi L
                    %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msubsup
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} tr
                    %mi R
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tr_R^X(\tr_L^Y(f))=\tr_L^Y(\tr_R^X(f)).
          %ul
            %li
              %strong Left pivoting
              For all
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi B
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi.mathscript 𝒞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A,B\in\mathscr{C}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:1\to A\otimes B
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} tr
                    %mi R
                    %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mtext id
                    %mi B
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msubsup
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} tr
                    %mi L
                    %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msub
                    %mtext id
                    %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tr_R^B(\text{id}_B\otimes f)=\tr_L^A(f\otimes \text{id}_A).
          %ul
            %li
              %strong Right pivoting
              For all
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi B
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi.mathscript 𝒞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A,B\in\mathscr{C}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi B
                    %mo →
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:A\otimes B\to 1
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} tr
                    %mi R
                    %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mtext id
                    %mi B
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msubsup
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} tr
                    %mi L
                    %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msub
                    %mtext id
                    %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tr_R^B(\text{id}_B\otimes f)=\tr_L^A(f\otimes \text{id}_A).
          %p Graphically, these axioms can be stated as below:
          %p
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/pivoting-axioms.png", :width => "500px"}/
          %p
            It is from these axioms that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pivotal+categories"} pivotal categories
            get their name.
          %p
            A spherical trace category is a planar traced category in which the left and right partial traces agree. That is, for any endomorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:A\to A
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi.mathscript 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathscr{C}
            we have that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} tr
                    %mi R
                    %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msubsup
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} tr
                    %mi L
                    %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tr_R^A(f)=\tr_L^A(f).
          %h2#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The category of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite-dimensional+vector+spaces"} finite-dimensional vector spaces
                is traced monoidal. In skeletal form, the objects are natural numbers regarded as dimensions, morphisms are matrices regarded as linear maps, and tensor product is multiplication of the dimensions. The trace is a minor generalization of matrix trace:
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi Tr
                        %mrow
                          %mi m
                          %mo ,
                          %mi n
                        %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi i
                          %mo ,
                          %mi k
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ,
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi j
                          %mo ,
                          %mi k
                          %mo ′
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi i
                          %mo ,
                          %mi k
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ,
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi j
                          %mo ,
                          %mi k
                          %mo ′
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi m
                          %mo ×
                          %mi p
                          %mo ×
                          %mi n
                          %mo ×
                          %mi p
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mo ,
                          %mi j
                      %mo =
                      %munderover
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                        %mrow
                          %mi k
                          %mo =
                          %mn 1
                        %mi p
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi i
                          %mo ,
                          %mi k
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ,
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi j
                          %mo ,
                          %mi k
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      Tr_{m,n}^p((a_{(i,k),(j,k')})_{(i,k),(j,k')\in m\times p\times n\times p})_{i,j}=
                      \\sum_{k=1}^p a_{(i,k),(j,k)}.
              %p This is an instance of a trace in a compact closed category.
            %li
              %p
                The category of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/profunctors"} profunctors
                is also compact closed, hence traced monoidal (actually a bicategory, though). The trace of a profunctor
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi H
                      %mo :
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi B
                      %mo ×
                      %mi X
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi op
                      %mo ×
                      %mi A
                      %mo ×
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi Set
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H:(B\times X)^{op} \times A\times X\to \mathbf{Set}
                is the profunctor
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi Tr
                        %mrow
                          %mi A
                          %mo ,
                          %mi B
                        %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi H
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %msup
                        %mi B
                        %mi op
                      %mo ×
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi Set
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Tr_{A,B}^X(H):B^{op}\times A\to \mathbf{Set}
                given by the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coend"} coend
                formula:
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Tr
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi H
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi b
                      %mo ,
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %msup
                        %mo ∫
                        %mi x
                      %mi H
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi b
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi a
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Tr(H)(b,a)=\int^x H(b,x,a,x) .
            %li
              %p
                The category of sets and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+functions"} partial functions
                with the coproduct monoidal structure is traced, if we let the trace of a partial function
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo +
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi B
                      %mo +
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:A+X\to B+X
                be defined as follows. First, let
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi f
                          %mrow
                            %mi A
                            %mo ,
                            %mi B
                        %mo :
                        %mi A
                        %mo →
                        %mi B
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_{A,B}:A\to B
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mrow
                          %mi X
                          %mo ,
                          %mi X
                      %mo :
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_{X,X}:X\to X
                and so on be the evident partial functions that come from restricting the domain and codomain of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi Tr
                        %mrow
                          %mi A
                          %mo ,
                          %mi B
                        %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Tr_{A,B}^X(f)(a)=b
              %p
                if either
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mrow
                          %mi A
                          %mo ,
                          %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_{A,B}(a)=b
                or if there exists
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                such that
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi b
                        %mo =
                        %msub
                          %mi f
                          %mrow
                            %mi X
                            %mo ,
                            %mi B
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msubsup
                          %mi f
                          %mrow
                            %mi X
                            %mo ,
                            %mi X
                          %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi f
                          %mrow
                            %mi A
                            %mo ,
                            %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi a
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b=f_{X,B}(f_{X,X}^n(f_{A,X}(a)))
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a{:href => "#AbramskyHaghverdiScott02"} Abramsky, Haghverdi and Scott
              %p
                This is a basis for a categorical semantics of “while loops” in programming languages: we keep applying
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
                %em while
                it is returning in
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %li
              %p
                Consider the category of pointed
                = succeed "\u2018s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cpo"} cpo
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset"} poset
                with a bottom element
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊥
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bot
                and least upper bounds of
                = succeed "-chains." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ω
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+monoidal+category"} cartesian monoidal category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ×
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi B
                      %mo ×
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:A\times X\to B\times X
                be given as follows. First, for fixed
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a\in A
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mi a
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_a\in X
                be the least
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fixed+point"} fixed point
                of
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi π
                          %mn 2
                        %mo ⋅
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi a
                        %mo ,
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo :
                        %mi X
                        %mo →
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\pi_2\cdot f(a,-)):X\to X
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_2
                for the second product projection. Here the least fixed point exists by Tarski’s fixed point theorem, indeed
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mi a
                      %mo =
                      %munder
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋁
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 2
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo ,
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo ⊥
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_a=\bigvee_n(\pi_2\cdot f(a,-))^n(\bot).
              %p Then we let
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi Tr
                        %mrow
                          %mi A
                          %mo ,
                          %mi B
                        %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Tr_{A,B}^X(f)(a)=\pi_1(f(a,x_a))\in B
              %p
                In
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Haskell"} Haskell
                notation, where ‘let’ allows recursive definitions, we can more swiftly just write
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi Tr
                        %mrow
                          %mi A
                          %mo ,
                          %mi B
                        %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo =
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi let
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi b
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi in
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Tr_{A,B}^X(f)(a)\ \ \ = \ \ \ let\ (b,x)=f(a,x)\ in\ b
              %p
                (except that the built-in product types in Haskell are lifted products rather than categorical products:
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A,B)
                in Haskell is like
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ×
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\times B
                but with an extra bottom element added).
              %p
                This is an example of a cartesian category with a parameterized fixed point operator (
                %a{:href => "#Hasegawa97"}> Hasegawa 1997
                ).
            %li
              %p
                Let
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
                be a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cancellative+monoid"} cancellative commutative monoid
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mid
                the divisibility relation i.e. for every
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo ,
                        %mi b
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi M
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a,b \in M
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                      %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \mid b
                iff there exists
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c \in M
                such that
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi b
                        %mo =
                        %mi ca
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b=ca
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi M
                      %mo ,
                      %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (M, \mid)
                is a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preordered+set"} preordered set
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/thin+category"} thin category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi M
                      %mo ,
                      %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (M, \mid)
                becomes a monoidal category if we define
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi b
                      %mo =
                      %mi ab
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \otimes b = ab
                for every
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo ,
                        %mi b
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi M
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a,b \in M
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
                of the monoid. Note that tensor product of morphisms is obtained via the compatibility of the multiplication with divisibility: if
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                      %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \mid b
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi c
                        %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                        %mi d
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c \mid d
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ac
                        %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                        %mi bd
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ac \mid bd
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ac
                        %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                        %mi bc
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ac \mid bc
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                        %mi b
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \mid b
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                is an integral domain, then
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi R
                      %mo \
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mo ,
                      %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                      %mo ,
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (R \backslash \{0\}, \mid, \cdot, 1)
                is a traced (symmetric) monoidal category. Note that since
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi M
                      %mo ,
                      %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (M, \mid)
                is thin, all the coherence diagrams of the trace commute automatically.
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#RelationToCompactClosedCategories Relation to compact closed categories
          %p
            Every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+closed+category"} compact closed category
            is equipped with a canonical trace defined by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Tr
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi B
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi A
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %mi id
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi η
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi X
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mo *
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi id
                  %mi B
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi X
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mo *
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %mi id
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi ε
                      %mo ′
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Tr_{A,B}^X(f) = A \overset{id\otimes \eta}{\to} A \otimes X \otimes X^* \overset{f \otimes id}{\to} B \otimes X \otimes X^* \overset{id \otimes \varepsilon'}{\to} B
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi η
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta
            is a unit and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ε
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varepsilon'
            is a counit of appropriate
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunctions"} adjunctions
            (note that the symmetry makes the dual
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^*
            both a right and left adjoint of
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            Conversely, given a traced monoidal category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+construction"} free construction
            completion of it to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+closed+category"} compact closed category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Int
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Int(\mathcal{C})
            (
            %a{:href => "#JoyalStreetVerity96"}> Joyal-Street-Verity 96
            ):
          %p
            the objects of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Int
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Int(\mathcal{C})
            are pairs
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A^+, A^-)
            of objects of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi B
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi B
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A^+ , A^-) \to (B^+ , B^-)
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Int
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Int(\mathcal{C})
            is given by a morphism of the form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mo +
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msup
                    %mi B
                    %mo −
                  %mo ⟶
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mo −
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msup
                    %mi B
                    %mo +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A^+\otimes B^- \longrightarrow A^- \otimes B^+
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"} composition
            of two such morphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi B
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi B
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A^+ , A^-) \to (B^+ , B^-)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi B
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi B
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (B^+ , B^-) \to (C^+ , C^-)
            is given by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trace"} tracing out
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi B
                    %mo −
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B^-
            from the composite
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mo +
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msup
                    %mi B
                    %mo −
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mo −
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mn 1
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mo −
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msup
                    %mi B
                    %mo +
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mo −
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi g
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mo −
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msup
                    %mi B
                    %mo −
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mo +
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A^+ \otimes B^- \otimes C^- \xrightarrow{f\otimes 1} A^- \otimes B^+ \otimes C^- \xrightarrow{1\otimes g} A^- \otimes B^- \otimes C^+.
          %p
            Note that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            embeds
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fully+faithful+functor"} fully-faithfully
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Int
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Int(\mathcal{C})
            by sending
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi I
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A,I)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            is the unit object of the monoidal structure.
          %p
            Furthermore, a traced monoidal category that is
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star-autonomous+category"} autonomous
            must already be compact closed; see
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#HH13"} HH13
          %h3#in_cartesian_monoidal_categories In cartesian monoidal categories
          %p
            For a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+monoidal+category"} cartesian monoidal category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fixed+point"} fixed point
            operator satisfying certain properties (
            %a{:href => "#Hasegawa97"}> Hasegawa 1997
            ). When the category is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere+theory"} Lawvere theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/iteration+theory"} iteration theory
          %h3#adding_traces Adding traces
          %p
            Since any full monoidal subcategory of a traced monoidal category inherits a trace, not every monoidal category can be fully embedded into a traced monoidal category, and hence also not into a compact closed category. In fact, Plotkin observed that there are monoidal categories that cannot even be
            %em faithfully
            mapped into a traced monoidal category. This can be seen from the fact that a traced monoidal category has the “cancellation property” that if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msub
                    %mi id
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi X
                  %mo =
                  %mi g
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msub
                    %mi id
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\otimes id_{X\otimes X} = g \otimes id_{X\otimes X}
            then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msub
                    %mi id
                    %mi X
                  %mo =
                  %mi g
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} id
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\otimes id_X = g\otimes \id_X
            (since the latter is a trace of the former with one of the copies of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\otimes X
            transposed), but not all monoidal categories have this property.
          %h3#relation_to_feedback_categories Relation to feedback categories
          %p
            %span.newWikiWord
              Feedback categories
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/feedback+categories"} ?
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \;
            %a{:href => "#KatisSabadiniWalters02"} KSW02
            are a weakening of the axioms of traced monoidal categories. Feedback categories do not satisfy the yanking axiom and they only satisfy a weaker form of naturality on the object to be traced. Examples include some categories of automata.
          %h3#categorical_semantics Categorical semantics
          %p
            Traced monoidal categories serve as an “operational”
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            for
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"} linear logic
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Geometry+of+Interactions"} Geometry of Interactions
          %p
            In this context the free compact closure
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Int
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Int(\mathcal{C})
            from
            %a{:href => "#RelationToCompactClosedCategories"} above
            is sometimes called the
            %em Geometry of Interaction construction
            and denoted
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒢
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G}(\mathcal{C})
            (
            %a{:href => "#AbramskyHaghverdiScott02"}> Abramsky-Haghverdi-Scott 02, def. 2.6
            ).
          %h2#references References
          %p The concept was introduced in
          %ul
            %li#JoyalStreetVerity96
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andr%C3%A9+Joyal"}> André Joyal
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ross+Street"} Ross Street
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dominic+Verity"} Dominic Verity
              = succeed "," do
                %em Traced monoidal categories
              %strong 119
              (1996) 447-468 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/231966472_Traced_monoidal_categories"}> pdf
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/S0305004100074338"} doi:10.1017/S0305004100074338
          %p A good exposition is found in
          %ul
            %li#Selinger2011
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Selinger"}> Peter Selinger
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em A survey of graphical languages for monoidal categories
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://www.mscs.dal.ca/~selinger/papers/graphical-2up.pdf"} pdf
          %p A characterization of trace structures on cartesian monoidal categories is given in
          %ul
            %li#Hasegawa97
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Masahito+Hasegawa"}> Masahito Hasegawa
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Recursion from Cyclic Sharing: Traced Monoidal Categories and Models of Cyclic Lambda Calculi
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/viewdoc/summary?doi=10.1.1.52.31"} citeseer
          %p
            The equivalence between traces and parameterized fixed point operators appears as Theorem 3.1 (the author notes that this theorem was also proved independently by
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin+Hyland"} Martin Hyland
          %p
            Comprehensive discussion as a source for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Geometry+of+Interactions"} Geometry of Interactions
            is in
          %ul
            %li#AbramskyHaghverdiScott02
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Samson+Abramsky"}> Samson Abramsky
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Esfandir+Haghverdi"} Esfandir Haghverdi
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Philip+Scott"} Philip Scott
              = succeed "." do
                %em Geometry of Interaction and Linear Combinatory Algebras
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/viewdoc/summary?doi=10.1.1.24.7818"} citeseer
          %p
            The combination with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star-autonomous+category"} star-autonomous
            structure was discussed in
          %ul#HH13
            %li
              Tamás Hajgató and
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Masahito+Hasegawa"} Masahito Hasegawa
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  Traced
                  = succeed "-autonomous" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo *
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/volumes/28/7/28-07abs.html"} TAC
          %p Feedback categories and their connection to traced monoidal categories were discussed in
          %ul
            %li#KatisSabadiniWalters02
              Piergiulio Katis, Nicoletta Sabadini,
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bob+Walters"} Bob Walters
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item/ITA_2002__36_2_181_0/"} Numdam
